  "e.ch.note": "★The channels advance <strong>together</strong> — one dt, one old state, the closure iterated over all of them: the heat capacity is (3/2)V′n, so a density that moves beside the temperatures moves the very weight they are solved with, and splitting them creates (3/2)V′T·dn of energy every step. ★<strong>The current channel needs a traced metric</strong>: it reads ⟨|∇ρ|²/R²⟩, which four scalars do not determine, so on Miller geometry the switch is <strong>disabled</strong> rather than quietly ignored.",
  "e.ch.momentum": "Momentum channel ω<sub>φ</sub>",
  "e.torque": "Torque T<sub>φ</sub> [N·m]",
  "e.prandtl": "Momentum Prandtl number χ<sub>φ</sub>/χ<sub>i</sub>",
  "e.mom_note": "★<strong>The momentum channel is not a fourth channel of <code>core_march</code></strong>, because in the kernel it is not one: heat, density and current advance together (the heat capacity moves with the density) and momentum is its own entry, <code>solve_momentum</code>. So this is an <strong>operator split</strong> — same dt, same old state, the χ<sub>i</sub> this step's closure produced. What the split drops is the feedback of rotation on the heat channels <strong>within</strong> a step; on this tier that feedback runs through the E×B shear, and the turbulent closure is re-evaluated on its own cadence anyway. ★<strong>χ<sub>φ</sub> is prescribed, not solved</strong>: a momentum diffusivity is a TGLF output and this C port does not carry upstream's toroidal-stress weights (<code>closure.momentum_chi_phi</code> refuses rather than returning a zero). So what you set is a <strong>Prandtl number</strong>, χ<sub>φ</sub> = Pr·χ<sub>i</sub>, and it travels in the file. ★<strong>The torque rides the same deposition profile as the auxiliary power</strong>: on this tier that power is a neutral beam, and a beam deposits both energy and momentum where it stops. A second pair of sliders would suggest the two can be set independently, and this tier has no ray tracing (that is T-M2). ★<strong>⟨R²⟩ is on this ladder now</strong> (T-M8): the momentum equation's capacity is V′ n m ⟨R²⟩, and while the ladder carried ⟨|∇ρ|²⟩ and ⟨|∇ρ|²/R²⟩ only, the capacity stood in the surface's own major radius squared, R<sub>maj</sub>(ρ)² — the first term of that average, short by O((a/R)²). Both metric carriers return the average itself now: the traced ladder from <code>equilibrium_ladder</code>'s own contour integrals, the Miller tier from <code>geo_surface</code>, against the same volume weight in both; on a circular surface the two differ by exactly 1.5 r² — measured on this page's EAST Miller surfaces, <strong>9.8 %</strong> at the edge, which moved ω(0) from 71.19 to 71.52 krad/s when it was wired in. <strong>Both columns travel in the file</strong> (<code>fylite:r2</code> and <code>fylite:rmaj2</code>) so the gap is yours to divide, and a metric without the column is <strong>refused</strong> rather than sent back to R<sub>maj</sub>². ★<strong>The edge is ω = 0</strong>: this tier has no scrape-off layer and no wall torque, so the boundary condition is \"the plasma is not rotating where the model stops\". ★<strong>And the E×B shear really does reach TGLF once it turns</strong>: <code>VEXB_SHEAR</code> comes from the kernel's <code>mapping::tglf_local</code> (which sign, which radius the derivative is against, which length normalises it, which sound speed — all four conventions live there), not respelled here. With the channel off the entry is 0, and that 0 is now a <strong>statement</strong> — no rotation was solved — rather than the placeholder it used to be.",
