  "e.lh_cap": "LH deposited power density and driven current density (on the ψ<sub>N</sub> shell grid); the band is σ<sub>j</sub>",
  "e.lh_note": "★★The whole chain is the kernel's, and it is <strong>one</strong> <code>lh_deposit</code> call: the per-surface accessibility limit <code>n<sub>∥,acc</sub> = ω<sub>pe</sub>/ω<sub>ce</sub> + √(1+(ω<sub>pe</sub>/ω<sub>ce</sub>)²)</code>, where each end of the launched band resonates by Landau damping, the damping layer between them, the Fisch-type <code>T<sub>e</sub>/n<sub>e</sub></code> current-drive weighting, the normalisation, and the σ<sub>j</sub> the band's two ends span. The resonant <strong>temperature</strong> comes from <code>lh_accessibility</code> (<code>lh_deposit</code> does not return it, and without it \"nothing resonated\" cannot be told from \"nothing was reachable\"); the local current-drive weight comes from <code>lh_efficiency</code>. ★<code>lh_resonance</code> and <code>lh_shape</code> are <strong>deliberately not bound</strong>: what they compute is already inside <code>lh_deposit</code>, and calling them again to print the answer beside it would give one number two hosts. This side only does the <strong>assembly</strong>, and it is the same assembly <code>fylite.scenario.model.lh.deposit</code> performs. ★<strong>The up-shift is the input that matters most here</strong>: EAST's launchers emit n<sub>∥</sub> ≈ 1.8–2.4, which by <code>T<sub>res</sub> = m<sub>e</sub>c²/(2ξ²n<sub>∥</sub>²)</code> resonates at 4.8–8.8 keV — hotter than the plasma — so a strict single-pass model (up-shift = 1) <strong>deposits nothing</strong>. Real LHCD damps only after multi-pass propagation up-shifts n<sub>∥</sub>; passing a <strong>range</strong> rather than a factor states that assumption instead of burying it, and lets σ<sub>j</sub> widen with the up-shift's own uncertainty. ★<strong>What is not here</strong>: ray tracing and Fokker-Planck (LSC / GENRAY-CQL3D are out of this tier), ion damping, the trapping correction to the efficiency, LH fast-electron pressure; the launched band (name, nameplate power and both ends of n<sub>∥</sub>) <strong>comes from the device description</strong> — the provenance line inside the box names it; what the description does not carry is the antenna's <strong>poloidal distribution</strong> (the DD's `position` is a per-shot signal), so the deposition is treated as a flux function. ★η<sub>CD</sub> is a <strong>calibrated coefficient</strong>, not a computed one: this page supplies it and writes it to the file, and it is reported <strong>beside</strong> the accessible fraction and <strong>never multiplied into it</strong> — one says whether the wave can get there, the other how much current it drives once it has.",
  "e.lh.replaces": "★With LH on, <strong>the I<sub>CD</sub> slider is disabled</strong> — the driven current is now <code>lh_deposit</code>'s <code>j_LH</code> (with a per-surface σ<sub>j</sub>), and a page offering a current-drive model beside a prescribed I<sub>CD</sub> is offering two answers to one question. ★The deposited power goes <strong>entirely to the electron channel</strong>: a lower-hybrid wave damps by electron Landau damping, this tier has no ion damping, and inventing an ion share would be a second deposition model.",
  "e.lh.needs_psi": "★LH deposition needs a <strong>ψ<sub>N</sub> map on the (R, Z) grid</strong> (shell volumes and mid-shell major radii) and <strong>|F(ψ)| per surface</strong> — accessibility is set by |B| ≈ F/R. The solved equilibrium and an imported g-file carry both; the analytic (Miller) tier carries neither, so it is <strong>disabled</strong> there rather than judging accessibility against a field nobody computed.",
  "e.lh.done": "★LH: {nl} system(s) launching {plaunch} MW → shell deposition <strong>{pdep} MW</strong> · driven current <strong>{ilh} kA</strong> · η<sub>CD</sub> = {eta} ×10<sup>19</sup> A/W/m² (<strong>a supplied calibration coefficient, not a computed one</strong>) · accessible volume fraction <strong>{reach}</strong> (<em>not</em> multiplied into η<sub>CD</sub>: one says whether the wave arrives, the other what it drives once it has) · effective n<sub>∥</sub> band {band} · resonance at {res}. {ns} shells · {cad}.",
  "e.lh.none": "★LH: {nl} system(s) launching {plaunch} MW, and <strong>nothing resonated anywhere, so nothing was deposited</strong> — that is a result, not a failure. The effective n<sub>∥</sub> band {band} resonates at {tres}, while this profile peaks at T<sub>e</sub> = {temax} keV: the wave needs a hotter plasma to damp in. Raise the up-shift (the resonant temperature goes as n<sub>∥</sub><sup>−2</sup>) or the edge temperature.",
  "e.lh.res_at": "ψ_N {lo} … {hi}",
  "e.lh.res_none": "none (that band end does not resonate in this profile)",
  "e.row.lh_p": "LH launched / shell-deposited power",
  "e.row.lh_i": "LH driven current I<sub>LH</sub>",
  "e.row.lh_acc": "LH accessible volume fraction",
  "e.row.lh_eta": "η<sub>CD</sub> (supplied calibration coefficient)",
  "e.row.lh_gap": "LH absorbed power: shell sum / ladder integral",
  "e.row.beam_calibre": "of which calibre (ψ<sub>N</sub> > {edge}, beyond the ladder)",
  "e.row.beam_disc": "the discretisation remainder (same domain, two rules)",
  "e.quad.note": "★★<strong>One absorbed power, two quadratures, two numbers</strong> (T-M11 / T-M14). The shell sum <code>shell_sum(p_dep, dV)</code> gives <strong>{shell} MW</strong> over the whole plasma (ψ<sub>N</sub> 0 → 1); the ladder volume integral gives <strong>{ladder} MW</strong> over <strong>the march's own metric</strong>, which stops at ψ<sub>N</sub> = {edge}; the difference is {gap}. <strong>Nothing is normalised onto anything</strong> — and since T-M14 nothing needs to be: the shell → ladder remap is a <strong>conservative per-interval integration</strong> (each node's dual cell is accounted against the kernel's own traced volumes, and the nodal value is solved back through the trapezoid weight), so the ladder integral is <strong>identically</strong> the shell sum minus the part outside the ladder, and the difference between the two numbers <strong>is the calibre itself</strong>: ① the <strong>calibre, {out} MW ({outpct})</strong>, deposited beyond ψ<sub>N</sub> = {edge}, where the ladder has no rungs — <strong>no refinement of any grid recovers it</strong>; it is T-M13's declared calibre and follows the outer-edge control. ② the <strong>residual discretisation difference, {disc}</strong> — floating-point noise. Before the conservative remap it was real: point-sampling the shell <em>averages</em> onto the nodes and then applying the trapezoid measured 3.16 % at 21 surfaces → 1.68 % at 61, shrinking but never gone; that part is now zero by construction. ★<strong>This bar's calibre is the ladder integral</strong>: τ<sub>E</sub>, Q and the power balance all use it, because it is what the march actually put in; the shell number is the beam's own account (by construction P<sub>inj</sub> × absorbed fraction).",
  "e.quad.na": "★★The gap between the shell sum and the ladder integral <strong>cannot be split</strong> this time: the ladder's outer boundary ψ<sub>N</sub> = {edge} does not fall inside the deposition shells, so \"the ring the ladder cannot reach\" is not defined. Both numbers are still printed in the table above, and the difference is still not normalised away.",
  "e.err.lh_nofield": "LH deposition needs a ψ_N map on the (R, Z) grid and |F(ψ)| per surface: the analytic tier has neither. Switch to the solved equilibrium or an imported g-file, or turn this off.",
  "e.err.lh_nopsi": "This equilibrium's ψ span is zero or non-finite, so no ψ_N map can be formed and the wave's shell table cannot be built.",
